This test checks the basic contract of IndexOf method in ArrayList. It observes the pre-state and post-state values like the Count of the ArrayList, existence of item in the list, and LastIndexOf result for the item within the array list. It also checks if the return value of IndexOf is non-negative, then it validates the post-state ArrayList contains the item.

Now it is able to perform random testing using properties of ArrayList class and validate the contract of IndexOf method. 
    
### Test the Add method:

To test the Add method, which can add an item to the list, we can create a test that verifies the post-state ArrayList equals the pre-state ArrayList plus the item being added. 

To do that, we will need to create an ArrayList instance, add an item, then call assertEquals to check that the two array lists are equal. If the two ArrayList instances are equal then it means that the add operation is successful. 

Here is an example of how it would look, in Java:

```java
@Test
public void addItem() {
   Object item = new Object();  // replace it with any item to add to the ArrayList

   // Create an ArrayList instance with some items
   ArrayList preList = new ArrayList();
   preList.add(new Object());
   preList.add(new Object());

   // Perform the add operation
   ArrayList postList = new ArrayList(preList);
   postList.add(item);

   // Add the item back into the preList for comparison
   preList.add(item);

   // Check that the two array lists are equal
   assertEquals(preList, postList);
}
```

This test will add an item into the ArrayList, then check if the pre-state ArrayList equals the post-state ArrayList. If they are equal, then it proves that the add method is successful. In this test case, an Integer item is added to an ArrayList of Objects, but you can add any object to an ArrayList.

By testing this way, we're effectively testing the post condition that the return value is in the list. 
If we are to use Pex, the Add operation might look like as follows, considering the pre-conditions and post-conditions:

```java
public void PEX_ArrayList_AddMethod_Test([PexAssumeSufficientEnumerationData]ItemType item, [PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList<ItemType> list )
{
    // PRE-CONDITIONS:
    int preCount = list.Count; 

    // OPERATION:
    list.Add(item);   

    // POST-CONDITIONS:
    int postCount = list.Count; 
 
     // An assertion that the size of the list increased by 1 after the add operation.
    Assert.AreEqual(preCount + 1, postCount); 

    // Assert that the list now contains the added item
    Assert.IsTrue(list.Contains(item));   

}
```
With Pex we can also generate a lot of different test cases for the Add method. By using a combination of pre-conditions and post-conditions we make sure our test checks all necessary scenarios. 

The important part is that with every method we want to test, we must think of all possible pre-conditions and post-conditions, and write a test case where we prove that the method behaves as expected under all those situations. This way, we can ensure that our ArrayList implementation is both correct and robust. 

NOTE: This tutorial is written in Java but you can apply the same principals to any object-oriented language that has an ArrayList class. Although some of the syntax may differ slightly. The main concepts are the same. –  Rajesh Kumar 

